{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790776815400-architect-mob-outstanding-decisions.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md collab/1790776815400-architect-mob-outstanding-decisions.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-30 14:11:07 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 21:11:07 +07\n\n# Architect mob, ROUND 1, fable seat\n\nRead-only; no file edited. Unlike my cell ruling, I executed a check this round: five programs on the AST walker, JVM only, through `tu/compile-and-run`. Claims about the other three VMs are still from reading. My first two attempts at that check launched the JVM test runner by mistake (`-M:test` and `-A:test` both carry the runner's main-opts); both were cut off at the timeout, so treat them as no test result.\n\n**What the run showed (AST walker, JVM):**\n\n| Program | Result | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `(assoc {:key 'forged-key :val 99} :effect :vm/store-put)`, then read `forged-key` | `99` | F1 confirmed: a `:pure` primitive performed a runtime-keyed store write. |\n| `(get {:d {:effect :vm/store-put :key 'k2 :val 7}} :d)` | `7` | F1 confirmed: reading effect-shaped guest data executes it instead of returning the map. |\n| Apply literal `{:type :closure :params [] :body {:type :literal …} :env {}}` | body ran | A data literal is executed as code. |\n| Same, with `:env {:yin.k/store-of :some-module}` and a `:vm/store-get` body | `nil`, no refusal | The forged store context was accepted. No real module store was present, so an actual cross-module read is by reading only (`engine.cljc:167-175`). |\n| Apply literal `{:type :reified-continuation :k nil :env {}}` to `5` | `5` | A forged continuation replaced the program's continuation. |\n\n## Decisions\n\n| D# | Decision | Rationale | Risk | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 | **Confirm copy-on-lift.** Sharing is preserved inside one lift; no cross-task shared cell. | The alternatives are a cell shared across tasks (violates \"no shared mutable state\"; needs a distributed protocol) or refusing every closure over a cell (makes stateful exports impossible). Copy matches module stores, which are instantiated per receiving task (`engine.cljc:832-863`), and cursor cells (`:606-618`). A program that wants shared state across tasks uses a stream. | Guest code expecting one shared counter across two requiring tasks silently gets two. A repeated `receive-module` re-lowers the slice while keeping the held store, so copies can multiply. Both belong to slice 2 and need a test there. | high |\n| D2 | **Keep `cell/*` for the surface; name the state `:heap` and the lifted table `:yin.k/heap`.** Never add a second meaning to `:yin.k/cell` or `:yin.k/cells`. | \"Cell\" is the word the owner and `yang.antlr.md` already use (\"store cells\", \"binding cells\"). The collision is only with engine-internal cursor vocabulary (`cell-for!`, `:yin.k/cells`), which no guest code sees. `box/*` would add a third word for one concept. | Readers of `engine.cljc` see \"cell\" in two senses. Mitigate with one docstring line, or rename the private `cell-for!` later. | med |\n| D3 | **Amend §8.1.** Baseline becomes a task heap of cells. State threading stays permitted as a per-frontend choice, not the baseline. | §8.1 contradicts §8.3, §8.5 and §9.4 of the same document. A threaded heap also conflicts with the owner's binding direction: under continuation-lowered control flow, every continuation argument would have to carry the heap. | Low. Doc only. | high |\n| D4 | **Fix F1 before the spike, as its own small commit immediately before cell slice 1.** Mechanism: an effect is a host type minted only by `module/make-effect`; `module/effect?` becomes a type test. | Confirmed by execution (table above). I withdraw my earlier \"gate on the callee's profile\" suggestion: it needs a reverse lookup per call. A host type is unforgeable because the engine consumes an effect immediately and never hands it to the program, so guest data can never be one. It is also a cheaper test than `map?` plus `contains?`. Doing it first means the three new cell constructors are written once. | About 37 `{:effect …}` literal sites in `src/cljc`, plus tests, need migrating. Must be verified on CLJS and CLJD. A host function profiled `:pure` that returns an effect is still not caught; that is a host-author bug, not guest forgery. | high |\n| D5 | **Accept: box every reassigned local for the spike.** No completion records. | Completion records contradict the owner's direction and double every call's return handling. A continuation captures its environment (`ast_walker.cljc:530-535`), so locals assigned between capture and invoke rewind unless boxed. Box-all is the only rule that is correct without liveness analysis. | Cost: with sealed refs as I first specified, every variable read re-hashes (see objection 1). Heap growth: nothing is reclaimed in slice 1. | high |\n| D6 | **Host error stays for now; no separate structural validation.** | Once continuations are unforgeable (D7), a malformed in-machine continuation cannot exist, and a continuation-shaped map is simply not applicable. Writing qualified defects for shapes that D7 makes impossible is throwaway work across four VMs. | Until D7 lands, a forged map produces an unqualified host error or, as shown above, silently redirects control. | med |\n| D7 | **Do not seal continuations like stream refs. Later, not before the spike.** Decide the direction now: programs are untrusted relative to VM control state, and the fix is host-typed in-machine values for closures and continuations together. | A stream ref's seal names a table entry. A continuation has none, so sealing means hashing its whole content per capture or registering every capture in a table that never shrinks. The gate's \"not a demonstrated new privilege boundary\" was too mild: the run shows a forged *closure* is already accepted, including a forged module-store context. Sealing continuations alone would leave that open. The portable encoding already separates VM values from wrapped literals (`engine.cljc:660-664`), so host types in-machine plus markers on the wire is coherent. | Until it lands, module-store isolation (linker §7.3 r7) is bypassable by any code in the task. Acceptable for a JVM-only spike running self-authored code; not acceptable before untrusted modules are linked. It is a four-VM refactor on hot paths and needs its own design doc. | med |\n| D8 | **Yes, remove both.** | The branch tip equals master (`8f9f90b0`). The worktree holds only three untracked collab files: two are identical to main's, and the third is an older copy of main's (main has the captured session id). Nothing is lost. | `git worktree remove` refuses with untracked files present, so it needs `--force`. That is a deletion, so the orchestrator should run it on this decision, not implicitly. | high |\n| D9 | **`git rm --cached` the 11 files and add an ignore rule for `collab/`. No history rewrite.** | `collab/` has no ignore rule today (`git check-ignore` matches nothing), which is how the 11 got committed and why every collab file shows as untracked. A credential-pattern scan of the 11 found nothing; they total 880K. Rewriting pushed history would break the three live worktrees for no benefit. | The files stay in history. If the owner prefers no trace in the repo, use `.git/info/exclude` instead of `.gitignore`; that is per-clone and the other worktrees share it. | high |\n| D10 | **Order: F1 fix → cell slice 1 → JVM-only Python spike as scoped.** D4 precedes the spike. D6 and D7 do not. | The spike's purpose is to test the owner's claim that ANTLR→AST lowering is straightforward; it needs cells and a safe effect path, nothing else. D7 is a separate track that must land before any multi-author module linking. | The spike's first finding may be performance (hash cost, effect slow path, heap growth). Record it as a measurement, not a blocker. JVM-only means no cross-host evidence for the grammar walker. | med |\n\n## Objections to my own cell ruling\n\n1. **Seal cost was glossed over.** `authentic-ref?` recomputes a SHA-256 on every check (`engine.cljc:287-299`, `:261-264`). As I specified it, every `cell/get` and `cell/set!` hashes, and box-all puts that on every variable access. **Amendment:** store the seal in the heap entry at allocation and compare on access, leaving one hash per `cell/new`. Even that may be too slow on CLJS and CLJD, where SHA-256 is software; measure in the spike.\n\n2. **Sealing cells is weaker than I implied.** With closures forgeable, a sealed cell ref protects little today. I still hold the decision, because it keeps one reference discipline and does not widen the hole. But if D7 lands as host types, cell refs should become a host type too and the seal disappears. Slice 1's ref shape is therefore provisional.\n\n3. **The threaded-heap rejection was overstated.** State threading gives something the heap cannot: rollback on re-entry. That is the right semantics for backtracking guests. It stays a valid per-frontend choice, which is why D3 says \"baseline\", not \"only\".\n\n4. **One claim in the ruling was under-tested.** I asserted that the four VMs share one effect path from reading. The register and stack VMs do call `engine/handle-effect` (`stack.cljc:441-455`, `register.cljc:469-485`), but slice 1's parity tests are the evidence, not my reading.\n\n5. **No reclamation is a larger gap than \"can wait\" suggested.** Box-all allocates one cell per reassigned local per activation. A long-running loop calling a function grows `:heap` without bound. It does not block the spike, but it should be the first item after it, ahead of lift/lower.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 14:03:08 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 21:03:08 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a0f29e-7b04-7891-a38f-274da87b9ad8\n\n\n| D# | decision | rationale | risk | confidence (high/med/low) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 | **Confirm copy-on-lift**, preserving aliases and cycles within the entire transferred value graph. Slice 1 refuses cell-bearing lifts. | Each receiving task owns an independent heap snapshot; sibling closures transferred together must retain their shared cell. This fits task ownership without cross-task mutation. Copying is a chosen transport contract, however, not the only possible consequence of the invariants. | Separate transfers produce separate identities. Repeated module linking must not split aliases between existing module stores and newly lowered exports. | high |\n| D2 | **Use `cell/new`, `cell/get`, `cell/set!`; state `:heap`, portable section `:yin.k/heap`.** | Binding cells describe the intended language semantics accurately. Explicitly distinguish heap cells from cursor cells; the existing cursor encoding need not change. | Implementers may accidentally reuse cursor-specific machinery or conflate the two identity spaces. | high |\n| D3 | **Amend §8.1 to an explicit task-owned cell heap**, updated through persistent VM-state transitions. | The heap remains immutable data at each transition; guest code no longer threads it through every function argument. Align §8.5 and §9.4 terminology, preserve logical identity, and state the initial portability limitation. | Describing this merely as “mutable heap” could invite host mutation or imply migration support that slice 1 lacks. | high |\n| D4 | **Fix before the executable spike, as a separate prerequisite to cell slice 1. Use callee-profile-authorized effect dispatch.** | Retain trusted callable profiles at registration/installation and build a task-local callable-identity lookup, avoiding per-call registry scans. Pure results always remain values. Effectful constructors must return a valid descriptor whose kind belongs to their declared effect set. Apply this uniformly across four kernels, including higher-order calls and module exports. | Existing code may depend on accidental shape dispatch. Reject conflicting profiles for the same callable identity; unknown callables must not gain effect authority from their results. | high |\n| D5 | **Accept cells and continuation lowering; strengthen the spike rule to box every function-local binding and parameter.** | “Every reassigned local” is insufficient without binding analysis: first assignment after capture, forward closure capture, and recursive local functions can also require shared locations. Allocate cells on function entry, initialize parameters, and use an explicit unbound sentinel for other locals. Keep control transfers as continuations. | More allocation and heap growth. Boxing alone does not implement `finally`, exception targeting, or cleanup on abrupt exits. | high |\n| D6 | **Require qualified defects for malformed in-machine continuations before the spike.** | Invalid authenticity, kernel identity, and control structure must fail before restoration, with consistent diagnostic categories across kernels. Validation must permit legitimate live closures and primitives; external plain-data gates cannot simply be reused. | Overly restrictive validation could reject valid captures; broad exception-catching could conceal engine bugs. | high |\n| D7 | **Seal continuations now, before the spike, using sealed references to private captured payloads.** | A guest-editable map must not supply authoritative control fields. Store each captured payload in explicit task state, issue a sealed reference, authenticate it, then restore the private payload. A seal over kind and ID alone is insufficient if invocation still trusts payload fields supplied alongside it. | Adds retained continuation state and reclamation work. This closes continuation forgery, not the separate issue of forgeable closure-shaped maps. | med |\n| D8 | **Approve retirement, but do not remove the worktree yet. Preserve its differing gate prompt first; then remove worktree and merged branch.** | Both worktrees point to `8f9f90b0`, and the branch is merged. However, byte comparison contradicts “collab synced”: two untracked files match, while `1790769087413-reviewer-continuation-invocation-gate.prompt.md` does not match the main-worktree copy. Preserve that variant before cleanup. | Immediate forced removal would discard unique task context. | high |\n| D9 | **Untrack the 11 files going forward; preserve local copies and history.** | Use a scoped `git rm --cached` for the verified tracked files and add an appropriate ignore rule. Durable architectural decisions should live in design documents. History rewriting is unnecessary for ordinary collaboration artifacts. | Untracking without ignoring permits accidental re-addition; removing artifacts before transferring authoritative decisions weakens discoverability. | high |\n| D10 | **D4 → D6/D7 → cell slice 1 → JVM-only Python ANTLR execution spike.** | All three safeguards precede guest execution. Slice 1 includes sealed cells, fresh task heaps, explicit lift/completion refusal, documentation, and four-kernel parity checks. Then use pinned grammars-v4 Python3 sources, a generic ParseTree-to-data walker, scope analysis, case-per-rule lowering to Universal AST, continuations, and the strengthened boxing rule. Parser-only exploration may proceed earlier. | Grammar coverage can exceed semantic coverage. Publish a bounded subset; reject unsupported rules explicitly. Defer generators, heap migration, and full Python compatibility. | high |\n\nThese are **Astra’s independent Round 1 decisions**, not a claim of two-seat consensus. No files were edited; no tests were run.\n\nObjections to the cell ruling:\n\n1. **The minimal boxing rule is too weak.** Python whole-block scope analysis is already required by [yang.antlr.md:1104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1104). Captures retain an environment at [ast_walker.cljc:530](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:530), and invocation restores it at [ast_walker.cljc:261](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:261). Consider `def f(): return x; x = 1; return f()` inside an enclosing function: `x` has only one assignment, but `f` needs the location established before that assignment. Box all local bindings for the spike; optimize only after binding and liveness analysis.\n\n2. **Cells are a sound baseline, not a proof that alternatives are impossible.** [§8.1:992](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:992) explicitly permits state passing, and [§8.10:1229](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1229) already requires an interop ABI. Those approaches have costs, but the ruling overstates their impossibility and inevitable allocation behavior. Likewise, a generator needs evolving identity, not necessarily this particular cell primitive. Choose cells because they simplify the selected lowering.\n\n3. **Continuation authenticity must protect the authoritative payload.** Current recognition checks only the tag at [engine.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:60). Existing resource seals cover only secret, kind, and ID at [engine.cljc:261](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:261). Copying that seal onto a continuation map while trusting its editable `:k` or `:env` would leave tampering possible. A private payload table gives the seal a stable, authoritative referent without hashing live host functions.\n\n4. **The cursor lift is not a cycle-safe implementation template.** [engine.cljc:606](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:606) constructs the cursor payload before recording its mapping. Recursive heap encoding must instead reserve the identity **before traversing contents**, and lowering must allocate identities before filling cells. Also, the repeated-link issue at [engine.cljc:847](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:847) is a correctness gate for slice 2, not merely a wart.\n\n5. **F1 requires preserving authority that current registration discards.** Host registration stores raw functions and profile addresses at [module.cljc:210](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:210); application then passes only the primitive’s result onward at [ast_walker.cljc:251](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:251). Replacing `effect?` alone cannot fix this. Carry trusted profile authority through callable resolution and invocation, and validate the declared effect kind.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
